---
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';

import ReferencePage from '../../components/reference-page.astro';
import { siteHref } from '../../lib/site';

const setup = 'uvx --no-config --isolated --python 3.14 --from code-standards code-standards setup\ncode-standards doctor';
const standalone = 'uv tool install repo-standards\nrepo-standards pull-request size . --base origin/main';
const manifest = 'enabled_rules = ["repository/artifacts/bespoke-iac-verifiers"]';
const workflow = `name: Pull-request size
on:
  pull_request:
permissions:
  contents: read
jobs:
  size:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: 10
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
          persist-credentials: false
      - uses: sarj-ai/repo-standards@9ec36427f34bab43589129d85d571dd476061c8e # v6.22.3`;
---

<ReferencePage title="Getting started" description="Set up local hooks, run a size check, and explicitly enable reviewed repository rules.">
  <article class="guide-page">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Setup</p>
      <h1>Getting started</h1>
      <p class="lede">Start with local feedback. Enable repository rules when you are ready.</p>
    </header>

    <nav class="command-index" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#install">1. Install</a><a href="#enable-rules">2. Choose rules</a><a href="#ci">3. Add CI</a>
    </nav>

    <section id="install" aria-labelledby="install-heading">
      <h2 id="install-heading">1. Install local checks</h2>
      <p>Install <a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/">uv</a>, then run from your Git repository. Sarj repositories use the shared Code Standards setup:</p>
      <Code code={setup} lang="bash" frame="terminal" wrap />
      <p>This installs staged repository checks, commit-message validation, and the pull-request history workflow in one environment.</p>
      <details class="disclosure">
        <summary>Standalone installation</summary>
        <p>For consumers using Repo Standards directly:</p>
        <Code code={standalone} lang="bash" frame="terminal" wrap />
        <p>Replace <code>origin/main</code> with your target branch. Upgrade with <code>uv tool upgrade repo-standards</code>; pin the package version in automation.</p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="enable-rules" aria-labelledby="enable-rules-heading">
      <h2 id="enable-rules-heading">2. Choose repository rules</h2>
      <p>Rules are disabled by default. <a href={siteHref('/rules/')}>Review a rule and its examples</a>, then add its stable ID to <code>.repo-standards/repository.toml</code>:</p>
      <Code code={manifest} lang="toml" frame="none" wrap />
      <Code code="repo-standards check ." lang="bash" frame="terminal" />
      <p>The manifest must be tracked in Git. Checks read committed <code>HEAD</code>; use <code>--staged</code> after staging both the manifest and files to check the index.</p>
      <p class="muted">The installed release supplies the implementation. IDs stay versionless. <code>--enable-rule</code> selects a rule for one run.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="ci" aria-labelledby="ci-heading">
      <h2 id="ci-heading">3. Measure pull-request size in CI</h2>
      <p>Save this workflow in <code>.github/workflows/pr-size.yml</code>. The size Action enables no repository rules; thresholds, labels, and approvals remain consumer policy.</p>
      <details class="disclosure">
        <summary>GitHub Actions workflow</summary>
        <Code code={workflow} lang="yaml" frame="none" wrap />
      </details>
      <p>Keep the full commit pins and complete Git history. Use <code>pull_request</code>; the size metric is not a security gate.</p>
      <p>To exclude generated files, commit the attribute in the trusted base revision:</p>
      <Code code="path/to/generated/** pr-size-excluded" lang="text" frame="none" wrap />
    </section>

    <aside class="callout" aria-labelledby="next-step">
      <h2 id="next-step">Next: prepare a pull request</h2>
      <p><a href={siteHref('/git-policies/')}>Git policies</a> covers headers, descriptions, and history. Use the <a href={siteHref('/cli/')}>CLI reference</a> for command options.</p>
    </aside>
  </article>
</ReferencePage>
